#include <stdlib.h>
typedef struct Node {
    int data; struct Node *next;
} Node;
/* Returns 1 on success, 0 if allocation fails. */
int push(Node **stack, int x) {
    Node *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
    if (!p) return 0;
    p->data = x;
    if (*stack == NULL) {
        p->next = p;
        *stack = p;
    } else {
        p->next = (*stack)->next;
        (*stack)->next = p;
    }
    return 1;
}

circular-push.text:
Circular list and stack push
Understand the idea
In a circular list, the last node points back to the first. A stack inserts and removes at its top.
Follow the pointer updates in the stated order.


Circular list
The last node points to the first node instead of NULL. A stack must insert and remove from the same logical end to provide LIFO.


Pointer convention
Use stack as a tail pointer. The top node is stack->next. This makes push O(1). An empty stack is NULL; a one-node stack points to itself. This convention is chosen explicitly because the question does not specify it.


C function
#include <stdlib.h>
typedef struct Node {
    int data; struct Node *next;
} Node;
/* Returns 1 on success, 0 if allocation fails. */
int push(Node **stack, int x) {
    Node *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
    if (!p) return 0;
    p->data = x;
    if (*stack == NULL) {
        p->next = p;
        *stack = p;
    } else {
        p->next = (*stack)->next;
        (*stack)->next = p;
    }
    return 1;
}

Effect
For top 10 → 20 → tail 30, push(5) creates top 5 → 10 → 20 → 30 → 5. The tail remains 30. Call push(&stack,5). Allocation failure leaves the old list unchanged.

