circular.text:
Circular linked list
Understand the idea
A circular linked list has no null link at its end: the last node points back to the first.
Stop a traversal when you return to the starting node.


Structure
A circular linked list links its last node back to the first.
An empty list can be represented by NULL; a one-node list points to itself.


Traversal
if (head != NULL) {
    Node *p = head;
    do {
        printf("%d ", p->data);
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != head);
}

Example and operations
10 → 20 → 30 → 10 repeats in a circle. Stop after returning to head, not at NULL.
With a tail pointer, head=tail->next. To append a new node: new->next=tail->next; tail->next=new; tail=new. For an empty list, set new->next=new and tail=new.


Uses and cost
Round-robin scheduling and circular queues repeatedly cycle through items.
Traversal takes O(n); insertion after a known node takes O(1).
Broken links or an incorrect stopping condition can cause an infinite loop.

