Linked list vs array: expected option E
All of the above under the usual fixed-array/singly-linked-list comparison.
Arrays have good cache locality because elements are next to one another.
Linked-list insertion/deletion needs only link updates when the relevant node/predecessor is known; searching still costs time.
A typical linked list does not support O(1) random indexed access.
A fixed array has a chosen capacity, while a linked list can allocate/free nodes as needed. Dynamic arrays also exist, and allocation can fail.


